[center][color=#00f0ff][size=6][b]NC ZONING BOARD[/b][/size][/color]
[color=#8892b0]NIGHT CORP // URBAN PLANNING DIVISION[/color]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]INSTRUMENT NC-ZB-01 - THE SURVEY REGISTER[/b][/size][/color]
[i]NC Zoning Board - Core: the registry, in game, for other mods to build on.[/i][/center]
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]0. NATURE OF THIS INSTRUMENT[/b][/size][/color]
[b]NC Zoning Board - Core[/b] is a framework - a modder's resource. It fetches the [b]NC Zoning Board[/b] registry, an interactive map of Cyberpunk 2077 location mods, and exposes it in game as a read-only API that other mods can use: each mapped location's name, authors, world coordinates, category, tags, and district.

[color=#ffb300][b]It has no menu and does nothing on its own.[/b][/color] You only need it if another mod lists it as a requirement. Installing it by itself has no visible effect.
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]1. THE WIDER PROJECT[/b][/size][/color]
The Core is the in-game half of [b]NC Zoning Board[/b], the interactive map of Cyberpunk 2077 location mods.
[list]
[*][b]Explore the map:[/b] [url=https://nczoning.net]nczoning.net[/url]
[*][b]Join the community:[/b] [url=https://discord.gg/sc4yEx2fNf]Locations Hub Discord[/url]
[/list]
[center][b]Showcase[/b]
[youtube]30Daism2E-c[/youtube]
[b]Trailer[/b]
[youtube]LJVJ2lN4aQ0[/youtube][/center]
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]2. DO I NEED TO FILE THIS?[/b][/size][/color]
Only if a mod you want tells you to. The Core is a shared dependency, like Codeware or RED4ext: it sits in the background and provides data to the mods that ask for it.

[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]3. WHAT IT DOES UNDER THE HOOD[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*]Downloads the registry once per game session over HTTPS.
[*]Caches it on disk, so it works offline after the first online launch and revalidates efficiently (no re-download when nothing changed).
[*]Serves the data to redscript and CET Lua mods through a small, stable API.
[*]Tells a consumer mod which location mods you already have installed, so it can mark what is present and what is missing. This part needs Cyber Engine Tweaks; without it, every location reads as "unknown".
[/list]
It makes [b]no[/b] changes to your game: no writes, no telemetry, no position tracking. Its only piece of UI is the alert that tells you the registry failed to load.
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]4. CONDITIONS OF ISSUE[/b][/size][/color]
[b]Required:[/b]
[list]
[*]RED4ext
[*]redscript
[*]Codeware
[*]RedData
[*]RedFileSystem
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/31920]RedLogger[/url]
[/list]
[i]RedLogger is what the Core writes its log to. It gives this mod its own file at [font=Courier New]r6\logs\mods\NCZoningCore__<date_time>.log[/font], one per session, keeping the five most recent. That file is what to attach to a bug report.[/i]

[b]Optional:[/b]
[list]
[*]RedHttpClient
[*]Cyber Engine Tweaks
[/list]
[i]Cyber Engine Tweaks is [b]not[/b] required by the Core itself. It is needed for installed-mod detection, and by any consumer mod that is itself a CET Lua mod.[/i]
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]5. RedHttpClient IS OPTIONAL[/b][/size][/color]
RedHttpClient is the plugin that lets the game make HTTPS requests, and it is the only reason the Core touches the network at all. It is a [b]soft dependency[/b]: the Core installs and runs without it.

[b]With RedHttpClient.[/b] The registry downloads itself once per session and keeps itself current. Nothing for you to do.

[b]Without RedHttpClient.[/b] The Core never opens a socket. It reads the registry from a file you download yourself, and treats it as permanently out of date, because it has no way to check. You re-download whenever you want fresher data.

[color=#ffb300]If you skip RedHttpClient and do not supply the file, the mod has no data.[/color] It will tell you so on screen a few seconds into your session, rather than pretending the map is empty.
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]6. FILING THE SURVEY BY HAND[/b][/size][/color]
Open a terminal in your Cyberpunk 2077 folder and run:
[code]curl.exe -L "https://api.nczoning.net/v1/locations" --create-dirs -o "r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations.json"[/code]
That creates [font=Courier New]Cyberpunk 2077\r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations.json[/font]. That folder is fixed: RedFileSystem confines every mod to [font=Courier New]r6\storages\<mod name>\[/font], and the Core cannot read from anywhere else. There is only one file to fetch.

The output path is relative, so check it landed where you meant. Downloading a good file into the wrong folder looks like a complete success and does nothing:
[code]dir r6\storages\NCZoningCore[/code]
You should see [font=Courier New]locations.json[/font], around 260 KB (it grows with the registry; [color=#ffb300]anything under ~10 KB means you saved an error page, not the data[/color]).

[b]Vortex, or a manual install:[/b] nothing special to do. The command above is all you need.

[b]Mod Organizer 2:[/b] the game directory is virtualised, so instead either run the command from your [font=Courier New]overwrite\[/font] folder, or create [font=Courier New]r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations.json[/font] inside a mod folder and let MO2 deploy it (which keeps it out of overwrite and lets you uninstall it cleanly).

The overwrite route is the same place the mod writes to anyway: with RedHttpClient installed, the game's write passes through the virtual filesystem and MO2 drops it in [font=Courier New]overwrite\r6\storages\NCZoningCore\[/font]. So if you add RedHttpClient later, it refreshes the file you already put there instead of creating a second copy elsewhere.

[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]6a. OPTIONAL - A ONE-CLICK REFRESH[/b][/size][/color]
[i]For anyone comfortable creating a small batch file. Entirely optional - the single command above is all you actually need.[/i]

Save this as [font=Courier New]refresh-nczoning-data.bat[/font]. Nothing in it is specific to your machine: [font=Courier New]%~dp0[/font] is the file's own folder, so it works out its own paths and there is nothing to edit.

[code]@echo off
set "DEST=%~dp0overwrite\r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations.json"
set "LOG=%~dp0refresh-nczoning-data.log"
echo [%DATE% %TIME%] fetching NC Zoning Board registry> "%LOG%"
curl.exe -L --fail --create-dirs -o "%DEST%" "https://api.nczoning.net/v1/locations" >> "%LOG%" 2>&1
if errorlevel 1 (
  echo   FAILED - see %LOG%
  pause
  exit /b 1
)
for %%A in ("%DEST%") do set SIZE=%%~zA
echo   OK - wrote %SIZE% bytes to:
echo   %DEST%
if %SIZE% LSS 10000 echo   WARNING: too small - probably an error page, not the data.
pause[/code]

[list]
[*][b]Mod Organizer 2:[/b] put it in your MO2 [b]instance[/b] folder - the one containing [font=Courier New]mods\[/font], [font=Courier New]profiles\[/font] and [font=Courier New]overwrite\[/font] - then add it under [font=Courier New]Tools > Executables[/font] with [b]no arguments at all[/b], and run it from the dropdown.
[*][b]Vortex or a manual install:[/b] put it in your Cyberpunk 2077 folder, and change the [font=Courier New]DEST[/font] line to [font=Courier New]set "DEST=%~dp0r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations.json"[/font] - there is no [font=Courier New]overwrite\[/font] involved. Then just double-click it.
[/list]

Use the batch file rather than pointing MO2 straight at [font=Courier New]curl.exe[/font]. Put the whole command in a launcher's Arguments box and it often does nothing at all - no file, no error, no clue, because the console window closes the instant it exits. The batch file writes a log beside itself on every run and holds the window open, so a failure tells you what went wrong.

[i]This is a convenience, not a substitute for RedHttpClient.[/i] The mod still treats the file as a permanent snapshot and will say so. Re-run the button when you want newer data.
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]7. INSTALLATION[/b][/size][/color]
[list=1]
[*]Install the requirements above.
[*]Download [b]NC Zoning Board - Core[/b] and extract the [font=Courier New]r6[/font] folder into your Cyberpunk 2077 root directory.
[i]Final path should look like:[/i] [font=Courier New]Cyberpunk 2077\r6\scripts\NCZoningCore\[/font]
[*][i]Only if you skipped RedHttpClient:[/i] run the [font=Courier New]curl[/font] command above to download the registry.
[/list]
Works with mod managers, or install manually.
[line]
[color=#00f0ff][size=5][b]8. FOR MOD AUTHORS - BUILDING ON THE REGISTER[/b][/size][/color]
Build on NCZoningCore from redscript or CET Lua. The full consumer guide and worked examples for both are in the GitHub repository linked on this page.

In short: redscript consumers [font=Courier New]import NCZoning.Api.*[/font] (or guard with [font=Courier New]@if(ModuleExists("NCZoning.Api"))[/font] for a soft dependency) and subscribe to the [font=Courier New]NCZoning-DataReady[/font] / [font=Courier New]-DataRefreshed[/font] / [font=Courier New]-DataError[/font] events. CET Lua consumers call the [font=Courier New]NCZoningApi[/font] facade and observe [font=Courier New]NCZoningApi.OnDataReady[/font] for the data-ready signal.
[line]
